WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.165 --> 00:00:01.800
(gentle music)

00:00:01.800 --> 00:00:02.940
- Prior to the pandemic,

00:00:02.940 --> 00:00:03.870
we had a full house.

00:00:03.870 --> 00:00:06.550
It was wall-to-wall people
in here all the time.

00:00:06.550 --> 00:00:10.070
And then on March 16th, we closed,

00:00:10.070 --> 00:00:13.490
and we have had no customers
in here since then.

00:00:13.490 --> 00:00:14.400
- [Narrator] In mid-March,

00:00:14.400 --> 00:00:15.470
Gleason's boxing gym,

00:00:15.470 --> 00:00:17.980
like gyms and fitness
centers across the country,

00:00:17.980 --> 00:00:20.090
closed because of the pandemic.

00:00:20.090 --> 00:00:22.800
Now after roughly half a year on pause,

00:00:22.800 --> 00:00:25.400
Gleason's is preparing
to re-open its doors.

00:00:25.400 --> 00:00:27.900
- Instead of coming in at your whim,

00:00:27.900 --> 00:00:30.497
you have to come in
through appointment only,

00:00:30.497 --> 00:00:34.610
and the employees will all
have to have a COVID-19 test.

00:00:34.610 --> 00:00:36.040
Everybody that walks in

00:00:36.040 --> 00:00:38.820
will have to immediately
use hand sanitizer.

00:00:38.820 --> 00:00:41.170
We'll also take their temperature.

00:00:41.170 --> 00:00:42.750
- [Narrator] New York State
has provided gym owners

00:00:42.750 --> 00:00:45.810
with 35 pages of protocols
they'll have to follow

00:00:45.810 --> 00:00:47.380
if they wanna reopen.

00:00:47.380 --> 00:00:48.800
But it still isn't clear

00:00:48.800 --> 00:00:50.930
what the new normal for
gyms will look like.

00:00:50.930 --> 00:00:53.270
Will clients return to their old routines,

00:00:53.270 --> 00:00:55.320
or will the pandemic have a lasting impact

00:00:55.320 --> 00:00:56.900
on our workout habits?

00:00:56.900 --> 00:00:59.730
Simply put, how might
the coronavirus pandemic

00:00:59.730 --> 00:01:02.093
transform the fitness industry?

00:01:02.093 --> 00:01:04.150
(gentle music)

00:01:04.150 --> 00:01:08.500
The American fitness industry
has been upended by COVID-19.

00:01:08.500 --> 00:01:10.880
24 Hour Fitness and Gold's Gym

00:01:10.880 --> 00:01:12.720
filed for Chapter 11 bankruptcy

00:01:12.720 --> 00:01:15.400
after missing out on membership dues.

00:01:15.400 --> 00:01:17.430
The parent company of
New York Sports Clubs

00:01:17.430 --> 00:01:18.870
and Lucille Roberts gyms

00:01:18.870 --> 00:01:21.914
have also warned of
potential bankruptcies.

00:01:21.914 --> 00:01:25.951
- COVID-19 pandemic has
created severe shock

00:01:25.951 --> 00:01:29.540
to this industry, to operators.

00:01:29.540 --> 00:01:33.630
This is industry that derive
majority of the revenue

00:01:33.630 --> 00:01:35.450
from monthly membership, right?

00:01:35.450 --> 00:01:39.720
So one, the gyms were forced
to close in mid-March.

00:01:39.720 --> 00:01:41.830
The revenue stream had gone.

00:01:41.830 --> 00:01:44.940
It was really zero revenue coming in,

00:01:44.940 --> 00:01:48.370
while you still have
huge lease obligations,

00:01:48.370 --> 00:01:50.350
as well as the labor cost.

00:01:50.350 --> 00:01:53.900
So the company, all of a
sudden, went into severe stress.

00:01:53.900 --> 00:01:55.490
- [Narrator] According to O'Brien,

00:01:55.490 --> 00:01:56.540
before the pandemic,

00:01:56.540 --> 00:02:00.260
many chains borrowed heavily
to expand aggressively.

00:02:00.260 --> 00:02:02.150
- The insurers that
cover a majority of them

00:02:02.150 --> 00:02:04.040
are owned by private equity firms,

00:02:04.040 --> 00:02:06.550
and the private equity
firms are smart money,

00:02:06.550 --> 00:02:10.140
and they're attracted to industry
for two reasons primarily.

00:02:10.140 --> 00:02:12.820
Number one is the recurring
nature of the membership.

00:02:12.820 --> 00:02:14.360
You have a very reliable,

00:02:14.360 --> 00:02:17.860
steady stream of monthly
revenue coming in;

00:02:17.860 --> 00:02:20.200
and number two, there is this long-term,

00:02:20.200 --> 00:02:22.730
fundamental demographic shift,

00:02:22.730 --> 00:02:24.760
a focus of fitness and wealth.

00:02:24.760 --> 00:02:28.140
As much as we think
that people go to gyms,

00:02:28.140 --> 00:02:30.970
that, in terms of percentage of people

00:02:30.970 --> 00:02:33.500
actually belonging to gym memberships,

00:02:33.500 --> 00:02:36.870
it's still in the low 20% for the US.

00:02:36.870 --> 00:02:39.050
So there's still a huge growth trend.

00:02:39.050 --> 00:02:40.950
- [Narrator] But the pandemic
has changed the math.

00:02:40.950 --> 00:02:43.440
- You're stuck with very expensive leases

00:02:43.440 --> 00:02:45.520
that worked in the old time,

00:02:45.520 --> 00:02:47.430
but doesn't really work in the new normal.

00:02:47.430 --> 00:02:49.080
It's a huge problem.

00:02:49.080 --> 00:02:50.370
- [Narrator] One gym that has been spared

00:02:50.370 --> 00:02:52.720
the worst of the pandemic
is Planet Fitness,

00:02:52.720 --> 00:02:53.673
the biggest chain in the country.

00:02:53.673 --> 00:02:56.250
- There's not many other
businesses out there

00:02:56.250 --> 00:02:58.960
that through this had zero
revenue since we shut down.

00:02:58.960 --> 00:03:00.780
There's not takeout food,
there's no delivery service.

00:03:00.780 --> 00:03:03.020
This whole industry had zero revenue.

00:03:03.020 --> 00:03:05.810
You know, luckily, we're franchise system,

00:03:05.810 --> 00:03:08.440
lotta sophisticated franchisees,
multi-store operators,

00:03:08.440 --> 00:03:09.690
which has really helped us.

00:03:09.690 --> 00:03:12.150
They might have five,
10, or 25 plus stores

00:03:12.150 --> 00:03:14.220
in multiple states,
which has also helped us.

00:03:14.220 --> 00:03:15.620
We might have some
closed in New York still,

00:03:15.620 --> 00:03:17.720
but have clubs in Florida that
have been open for a while,

00:03:17.720 --> 00:03:19.884
so it's really helped
us along through this.

00:03:19.884 --> 00:03:21.745
(gentle music)

00:03:21.745 --> 00:03:23.110
- [Announcer] A chance to grow stronger.

00:03:23.110 --> 00:03:24.650
- [Narrator] One of
the few areas of growth

00:03:24.650 --> 00:03:26.964
in the fitness industry
during the pandemic

00:03:26.964 --> 00:03:29.320
has been at-home classes.

00:03:29.320 --> 00:03:31.850
With gyms closed, many Americans scrambled

00:03:31.850 --> 00:03:34.270
to create their own at-home gyms,

00:03:34.270 --> 00:03:35.770
leading to a shortage of equipment

00:03:35.770 --> 00:03:37.670
like kettlebells and rowing machines.

00:03:37.670 --> 00:03:39.045
(gentle inspirational music)
(woman gasps)

00:03:39.045 --> 00:03:40.090
- [Woman] A Peleton?

00:03:40.090 --> 00:03:41.063
- [Trainer] Give it up
for our first time riders.

00:03:41.063 --> 00:03:43.390
- All right, first ride.

00:03:43.390 --> 00:03:45.410
- The other part of the industry trend

00:03:45.410 --> 00:03:47.660
we've seen in the last few years

00:03:47.660 --> 00:03:50.930
is technology is really
transforming this industry.

00:03:50.930 --> 00:03:53.060
It started with companies like Peleton.

00:03:53.060 --> 00:03:54.250
About a few years ago,

00:03:54.250 --> 00:03:55.730
they started offering at-home.

00:03:55.730 --> 00:03:57.770
You can buy a Peleton bike,

00:03:57.770 --> 00:04:01.130
and pay a monthly subscription, about $40,

00:04:01.130 --> 00:04:03.110
and have access to their on-demand

00:04:03.110 --> 00:04:04.990
as well as live classes.

00:04:04.990 --> 00:04:08.360
So this pandemic with
the lockdown measures,

00:04:08.360 --> 00:04:10.560
stay at home measures, remote working,

00:04:10.560 --> 00:04:14.063
we've accelerated this
at home workout trend.

00:04:14.063 --> 00:04:15.590
- I mean, home fitness is not new.

00:04:15.590 --> 00:04:17.250
It's been here since Richard
Simmons and Jane Fonda.

00:04:17.250 --> 00:04:19.060
Then it was Tae Bo with Billy Blanks,

00:04:19.060 --> 00:04:20.250
and then it was P90X,

00:04:20.250 --> 00:04:21.410
that it's always been here.

00:04:21.410 --> 00:04:22.243
I do, however, think

00:04:22.243 --> 00:04:26.740
that this has accelerated
the adoption of digital,

00:04:26.740 --> 00:04:27.810
whether it's in-club or out-of-club,

00:04:27.810 --> 00:04:30.220
I think people now realize
that they can utilize this

00:04:30.220 --> 00:04:32.760
and learn how to exercise better,

00:04:32.760 --> 00:04:35.400
get the most of their
membership in-club and at home.

00:04:35.400 --> 00:04:37.580
(gentle music)
- Back in Brooklyn,

00:04:37.580 --> 00:04:40.090
Bruce Silverglade remains
committed to Gleason's,

00:04:40.090 --> 00:04:42.980
despite the challenges
he's faced during lockdown.

00:04:42.980 --> 00:04:46.000
- The pandemic has taken
a tremendous toll on me,

00:04:46.000 --> 00:04:47.820
emotionally and physically.

00:04:47.820 --> 00:04:52.330
The monthly expense
has been about $36,000.

00:04:52.330 --> 00:04:54.590
The monthly income has been zero.

00:04:54.590 --> 00:04:57.460
So the only that I've
been able to stay afloat

00:04:57.460 --> 00:05:00.370
is to use some of my personal monies

00:05:00.370 --> 00:05:02.850
and to borrow money from the bank.

00:05:02.850 --> 00:05:04.570
And this has been

00:05:05.750 --> 00:05:09.650
something that is new
to me and to Gleason's.

00:05:09.650 --> 00:05:11.580
Prior to the pandemic,

00:05:11.580 --> 00:05:13.150
I was debt-free.

00:05:13.150 --> 00:05:15.833
Big expense, again, was
my rent and my payroll.

00:05:17.100 --> 00:05:19.360
Other than that, I had no bills.

00:05:19.360 --> 00:05:24.360
And now, when this is over
and I get back to business,

00:05:24.641 --> 00:05:27.670
(clears throat) I'm probably
gonna have a $200,000 debt.

00:05:27.670 --> 00:05:29.870
(gentle music)

00:05:29.870 --> 00:05:30.750
- [Narrator] Despite Gleason's

00:05:30.750 --> 00:05:32.800
being a small, independent gym,

00:05:32.800 --> 00:05:35.830
Silverglade is confident it will survive.

00:05:35.830 --> 00:05:38.720
But few independent
gyms are like Gleason's.

00:05:38.720 --> 00:05:40.380
The Brooklyn-based boxing gym

00:05:40.380 --> 00:05:43.200
is one of the best known
boxing gyms in the country,

00:05:43.200 --> 00:05:44.450
if not the world.

00:05:44.450 --> 00:05:45.640
- I don't wanna get sick, okay?

00:05:45.640 --> 00:05:47.410
I'm 74 years old,

00:05:47.410 --> 00:05:48.940
I have diabetes.

00:05:48.940 --> 00:05:49.870
So there's no way in the world

00:05:49.870 --> 00:05:51.270
I wanna have people come in here

00:05:51.270 --> 00:05:55.280
and inflict COVID-19 on anyone or on me.

00:05:55.280 --> 00:05:56.680
Gleason's will not close,

00:05:56.680 --> 00:05:58.670
I won't allow it to close.

00:05:58.670 --> 00:06:01.163
But I'm gonna have a huge debt.

